# L5R 5E - PATH OF WAVES: HIROSAKA (LORE)

**Version:** 0.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Path of Waves (Chapter 5)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## HOW TO USE THIS TOWN

Much larger than villages but smaller than cities, towns in Rokugan bridge the vital labor of commoners to the great works of the powerful aristocrats. Hirosaka -- meaning "broad hill" -- is a typical Rokugani town, offering a ready-made setting representing the sort of smaller urban area a party of samurai is likely to encounter. While governed by one of the Imperial families, it is designed to be easily translatable to whatever place in the Empire best suits a campaign.

Hirosaka is deliberately generic. No detailed history is provided; instead, past events affecting its current form are noted so a GM can place it in any part of the Empire, adjusting it accordingly. Much of the chapter describes Hirosaka as an Imperial holding in Seppun family lands near Otosan Uchi, but suggestions for locating it in other regions are provided throughout.

---

## AN IMPERIAL HOLDING

Hirosaka is located a full day by foot north of Otosan Uchi, on the eastern bank of the River of the Sun. For nearly two hundred years, the town and surrounding lands have been administered by the Imperial families in general and the Seppun family in particular. This places Hirosaka in some of the most fertile and fully developed land in the Empire, accentuating its importance as a hub for travel, trade, and commerce.

The current population is about five thousand people, swelling and shrinking throughout the year in parallel with seasonal activities. During late summer and autumn, the population increases as harvested crops are brought in to be taxed, sold, stored, and shipped. In winter, the population decreases as commoners return to their rural homes.

Hirosaka is not primarily a military holding and has no exterior walls or large defensive works. Generally prosperous and orderly, it is not free of nefarious schemes and activities.

---

## USING HIROSAKA AS A GREAT CLAN HOLDING

### Crab Clan
Strong defensive works including an outer wall of wood and stone, several watchtowers (yagura), and at least two gates. The governor's palace is a fortified keep (tenshukaku). Dour and functional. Military authority overrides civil governance. A well-armed garrison is present. Most likely inland on dominating, defensible terrain.

### Crane Clan
Just as likely to be a port as inland. May show damage from recent tsunami. Resplendent with traditional, elegant architecture and beautiful gardens. Facilities related to performance and arts: theaters, schools, studios for sculptors and musicians, shops for kimono makers. A Daidoji holding sees significant commercial activity. The military is subordinate to civil authorities.

### Dragon Clan
Nestled in rugged or mountainous terrain, never a port. Little arable land, relying heavily on imported food. Large granaries and storehouses for winter. Most likely exports are minerals, stone, and related products. Lower proportion of samurai to commoners. Monks and holy people are more common and prominent. Any garrison or defensive works are small.

### Lion Clan
May sprawl in open country or sit in foothills. Larger military presence than most towns, with a strong garrison and more facilities such as barracks and dojo. Military authority is preeminent. Strong defensive works only likely if in a strategic location. Ancestor reverence and worship is particularly prominent.

### Phoenix Clan
Could occupy nearly any geographic setting. Traditional and elegant architecture incorporating prominent spiritual elements such as torii arches and numerous small shrines. Secular authorities share power with spiritual ones or may be subordinate to them. Military presence remains well in the background. One or more libraries host ongoing scholarly research. Increased presence of shugenja compared to other clans.

### Scorpion Clan
Could be in open lands, near mountains, or near forests. Traditional but somber architecture. Significant presence of commerce and activities related to art and performance; geisha houses and theaters are prominent. A more evident sordid side with gambling houses and opium dens operating openly. Civil authority generally appears predominant, though a strong military presence may be hidden.

### Unicorn Clan
Most likely set amid open plains. Architecture is much less traditional with evident gaijin influences from the Ivory Kingdoms or Burning Sands, such as domes and slender rounded spires. Trade in gaijin goods is relatively common. A significant transient population of nomadic Unicorn ebbs and flows with the seasons. Commoners are more visible and relaxed in the presence of samurai. The more westerly the location, the more likely gaijin are present.

### Minor Clans
GMs are encouraged to use the essential flavor of the Minor Clan together with general geographic and climatic conditions to inspire changes. For example, a Mantis Clan version would almost certainly be a port on a relatively rugged and isolated semitropical island.

---

## RONIN IN HIROSAKA

Ronin are found throughout Rokugan, and Hirosaka is no exception. In the default Seppun-controlled version, ronin are grudgingly accepted as long as they remain well behaved and reasonably out of sight. The situation would be similar in Lion, Crane, or Phoenix versions. In times of war, ronin are more prominent as mercenaries.

If set in Unicorn, Dragon, or Crab lands, ronin are more openly accepted and visible. In a Scorpion holding, they may even be welcomed -- though this is not altruism; to the Scorpion, ronin are useful but expendable pawns.

---

## GAIJIN IN HIROSAKA

Gaijin are viewed differently in different versions. In Crane, Lion, or Phoenix holdings, gaijin maintain a very low profile -- if accepted at all. Other clans are generally more welcoming, especially the Unicorn and among Minor Clans like the Mantis and Tortoise. In these versions, gaijin may be quite prominent, living in Hirosaka and involved in commercial, artisanal, and other day-to-day activities.

---

## GEOGRAPHY

### Local Geography

The River of the Sun rises in the southern reaches of the Mountains of Regret and flows southward through flat, open lands administered by the Seppun family, eventually passing through Otosan Uchi before emptying into the Bay of the Golden Sun. The river's periodic flooding has made the land along its course particularly fertile and productive.

Hirosaka is located on a broad series of low, rounded hills bordering the river -- hence the town's name -- allowing it to avoid the immediate effects of flooding. The surrounding lowlands are home to numerous farms. Farms more than a few hours away are generally vassals of other lords, such as Twin Blessings Village.

Only farms and villages in close proximity are governed by the daimyo. More remote settlements are governed by other lords, which can lead to tension over boundaries of authority and apportioning of taxes.

Farmed crops are not the only commodity passing through Hirosaka. Mineral resources such as iron, copper, and stone mined in the mountains are shipped southward, as is timber. All goods can be shipped farther south using river transport and a well-maintained Imperial road paralleling the river.

The surrounding flat terrain provides little defense in times of war, but Hirosaka relies on its location deep inside the Empire and its status as an Imperial holding for protection.

### Variant Geography: Coastal Setting

If located on the seacoast or a large inland lake, Hirosaka may become both a riverport and seaport. Additions include: a safe harbor (sheltered coves or bays), docks (piers, jetties, wharfs, and quays), warehouses for cargo, shipyards for maintenance and repair, and a port district with inns, rooming houses, sake houses, and gambling dens.

### Variant Geography: Mountainous Setting

If located in mountainous terrain, the riverport is lost and arable land diminishes. A large town in such terrain generally exists due to a strategic location or abundance of a valuable commodity such as jade or gold. Changes include: substantial food stores with large granaries, strong defense from steep slopes and cliffs reinforced by walls and watchtowers, and natural hazards including flash floods, avalanches, landslides, earthquakes, or volcanoes.

---

## RESOURCES AND TRADE

### The Regional Economy

The default version is located amid richly fertile farmlands lining the River of the Sun. The town functions as a regional hub where food and other commodities are gathered, taxed, and shipped onward.

The sequence of activities:
- **Commodities are produced:** Crops (rice, barley, wheat, millet) are grown and harvested. Farmers within a two-hour journey bring harvests directly. Other commodities -- iron, copper, stone, timber -- arrive from farther afield.
- **Commodities are taxed:** Taxes are collected as portions of each commodity at rates set by Imperial decree from the Emerald Champion. Collection is enforced by Emerald Magistrates and local magistrates. When all taxes are collected, anything remaining is allotted for sale.
- **Commodities are stored and distributed:** Food goes to granaries; minerals, stone, and timber to warehouses. Goods are batched for shipment by caravan or boat. Most goods ship to Otosan Uchi; some go to nearby holdings.

### The Local Economy

- **Food:** Every citizen receives an allocation based on social standing. Additional foodstuffs can be purchased.
- **Basic consumer goods:** Tatami mats, futons, baskets, pottery, utensils manufactured locally by heimin craftspeople.
- **Luxury goods:** Wall hangings, shoji screens, fine sake and teas produced by skilled craftspeople and samurai artisans.
- **Services:** Entertainment (Kabuki, poetry, music), inns, sake houses, tea shops, noodle stands, carpenters, masons, chirurgeons, herbalists. Much based on barter.

### The Underground Economy

Smuggling, extortion, protection rackets, and trade in illicit drugs (particularly opium) thrive in a hidden economy. In the Seppun version, the black market is very discreet; in Scorpion, Mantis, or Tortoise versions, it could be much more prominent.

---

## COMMODITIES PRICE TABLE

| Commodity | Low Price | High Price | Notes |
|-----------|-----------|------------|-------|
| Basic foods (rice, grains, vegetables, local fish) | 1-2 zeni | 3-5 zeni | Per person per day |
| Luxury foods (spices, imported fruits, seafood) | 1-2 bu | 3-5 bu | Per person per day |
| Basic drink (local sake, shochu, beer) | 2-3 zeni | 4-5 zeni | Per person per day, without excess |
| Luxury drink (premium/imported sake, imported wine) | 2-4 bu | 1 koku or more | Per person per day, without excess |
| Basic consumer goods (tatami mats, futons) | 5-7 zeni | 1 bu or more | Per item |
| Luxury consumer goods (shoji screens, artwork) | 1-2 koku | 3 koku or more | Unique/commissioned items negotiated |
| Tools (shovels, hammers, axes, picks) | 6-9 zeni | 1 bu or more | Per item |
| Utility items (rope, baskets, barrels, boxes) | 3-5 zeni | 6-10 zeni | Per item |

