## KEY LOCATIONS

Hirosaka is divided into quarters on the basis of function and social class: the Governor's Quarter, the Samurai Quarter, the Commoners' Quarter, and the riverport.

### The Governor's Estate
**Quarter:** Governor's Quarter

A sprawl of buildings enclosed in a walled compound on the highest ground in Hirosaka. The four-story governor's yashiki (mansion) offers a commanding view. The yashiki is one of the few buildings constructed largely of stone, functioning as a secondary defensive position in times of strife. It contains apartments for the governor and family, a large audience chamber for holding court and hosting social gatherings, and is relatively ornate and luxurious.

Remaining buildings include storehouses, servants' quarters, a small barracks for the personal guard, stables, a well-appointed guesthouse, a garden, a bathhouse, and a teahouse (chashitsu). Entry is strictly controlled. The governor's personal guard -- a small but well-equipped cadre of samurai and trusted ashigaru -- staff two gates at all times.

### The Municipal Court
**Quarter:** Governor's Quarter

A large wooden building where court is held most of the time. A raised dais provides a place for the governor or their designate. Unlike the governor's estate court, the municipal court is normally open to anyone. It also houses offices for the town's bureaucracy and the chancellor.

### The Magistrate's Station
**Quarter:** Governor's Quarter

A compact, utilitarian compound of stone buildings. The town's chief magistrate, Seppun Ishima, lives and maintains her office here. Fronting the building is the "White Sands of Justice," a small plaza covered in white sand where the accused are brought, their crimes declared, and judgment rendered. It is also used for duels, executions, and floggings.

Other buildings include a guesthouse for visiting magistrates, quarters for municipal magistrates, a jail, storehouses, and a stable. Rumored to be connected to the governor's estate by an underground passageway.

### The Temple Square
**Quarter:** Adjacent to the Samurai Quarter

The spiritual heart of Hirosaka. Each entrance is framed by a torii arch. One side is dominated by the Temple of the Fortunes, containing shrines to the Seven Great Fortunes and several Lesser Fortunes important to town life: Hamanari (Plentiful Meals), Hujokuko (Fertility), Kojin (Peaceful Homes), and Musubi (Fated Bonds).

Across from the Temple of Fortunes is a separate shrine to Inari, the Fortune of Fertility and Rice. A third side is dominated by the Temple of Shinsei, where Hinata, the abbot and most senior holy person, presides. Surrounding shrines vary by location.

### The Market (Trading Grounds)
**Quarter:** Commoners' Quarter, near the Samurai Quarter

The largest commercial space in town. An expansive open-air market where merchants erect stalls and pavilions. Commerce is subject to taxes or flat license fees. A portion is set aside for bulk commodities. During harvest season, this activity can monopolize much of the space. A shrine to Daikoku, the Fortune of Wealth, sits adjacent.

Wealthier, more-established merchants work out of shops and storefronts. Only in the Samurai Quarter is all overt commerce prohibited -- aside from sake houses, noodle shops, and geisha houses.

### The House of the Winding Path
**Quarter:** Samurai Quarter

A classic ryokan (traditional inn). Features a large entrance hall/common room, guest rooms with tatami flooring and futons opening onto a garden with a koi pond and arched bridge, a bathhouse, and function rooms for geisha entertainment. Entertainment is provided by hired performers.

The proprietor, a heimin woman named Aia, is entirely comfortable dealing with samurai. Her lord and owner is reputed to be a Crane samurai of considerable renown.

Other notable inns include Sun Glowing on Mist, The House of Evergreens, and The Lark's Song.

### Dwindling Fortunes (Gambling House)
**Quarter:** Riverport

A shabby sake house with a dingy common room. Cheap sake, beer, and shochu compose the menu. No food service; patrons are directed to a nearby noodle shop called Good and Plenty. Behind the common room is a gambling room reeking of tobacco and desperation. Games include Fortunes and Winds, Cho-Han, abufuda, Hanafuda, and even gaijin games like Moksha Patam from the Ivory Kingdoms.

The enigmatic gangster known as Tamiko maintains a stake in the action. The proprietor, a burly heimin named Three-Scar, employs a trio of scruffy ronin as staff.

Competitors include Dreams Fulfilled (an opium den and gambling house) and The Grinning Mujina (another sake den).

### The Granaries
**Quarter:** Between Riverport and Trading Grounds

Stoutly built storehouses vital to the town. After setting aside their own portion, heimin farmers bring harvests to be weighed and stored. Magistrates and bureaucrats assess taxes, then merchants compete to purchase the remainder. Adjacent facilities for drying, hulling, and milling rice and other grains.

### The Sake Works (Clouds above Hirosaka)
**Quarter:** Not specified

A small brewery producing two varieties of sake: Winter Jasmine (a clear, pale-golden fine brew sold primarily in Otosan Uchi as a premium product) and Clouds above Hirosaka (a cloudy nigori sake consumed mainly locally). Owned by the Scorpion samurai Bayushi Touma, who relies on his heimin brewmaster, Sana, to run operations.

### The Barracks
**Quarter:** Adjacent to Magistrate's Station, near Governor's Estate

Buildings officially intended for up to one thousand soldiers but rarely used. A small cadre of about a dozen Seppun samurai forms the core of the Hirosaka guard. In times of strife, commoners are drafted as ashigaru to fill the guard to about four hundred. Annual levy drills are conducted during summer. The compound includes barracks buildings, a command post, a small stable, a dojo, an open drill yard, an armory, and a smithy.

### The Hinin Encampment
**Quarter:** Separate, fifteen minutes north

A ramshackle place set apart from the rest of the town. Hinin handle corpses, trash, waste, vermin, and street cleaning. Not particularly squalid; the hinin seek to make the best lives they can. They live according to their own hierarchy and social codes. Regularly visited by monks, priests, and budoka deputies.

---

## KEY NPCs

### Seppun Sora, Ambitious Governor
**Conflict Rank:** Combat 3, Intrigue 6

A pudgy, middle-aged courtier consumed by thwarted ambition. Removed from the Imperial Court, he sees Hirosaka as political oblivion. Bitter, resentful, dour, and corrupt, primarily concerned with appearances. Has a weakness for fine food, drink, luxurious surroundings, and beautiful people. Little interest in governing details. Always seeks to meet visiting samurai to determine if they can help or hinder his return to court.

### Otomo Kazuko, Scheming Chancellor
**Conflict Rank:** Combat 2, Intrigue 7

A calculating woman in her late twenties who maintains an air of strict courtly propriety. Contrived to have herself assigned to Hirosaka to wait out coming chaos. Has blackmailed Sora into granting her both chancellor and chamberlain roles. Maintains an elaborate network of spies and informants. Retains a cadre of enforcers and assassins connected through layers of intermediaries. Extremely patient. May secretly be the criminal overlord known as Tamiko.

### Seppun Ishima, Conflicted Magistrate
**Conflict Rank:** Combat 7, Intrigue 3

A sturdy, brusque woman in her mid-twenties. Chief magistrate, head of law enforcement, commander of the town watch, overseer of firefighters, and chief tax collector. Determined and fundamentally honest, following her mother's legacy as an Emerald Magistrate. Deeply conflicted because she is aware of corruption between the governor and Kazuko but fears probing too deeply could taint her career. Effectively covering up suspected wrongdoing while telling herself she needs more proof.

### Hinata, Enigmatic Abbot of the Temple of Shinsei
**Conflict Rank:** Combat 2, Intrigue 5

A lean, wiry man in his late forties. Friendly, outgoing, with a ready smile and quick wit. Extremely charismatic, particularly amiable with commoners. His knowledge of the Tao of Shinsei is almost encyclopedic. Secretly a devotee of the Perfect Land Sect, coordinating sect activities in surrounding farmlands and villages. His packed ceremonies provide cover for sect meetings. Teaches commoners about the sect discreetly, only in the hinin encampment where no samurai is likely to step foot.

### Yuuto, Leader of the Commoners
**Conflict Rank:** Combat 2, Intrigue 4

An older man bearing scars and a pronounced limp from his days as an ashigaru. Intelligent, insightful, politically astute, but speaks with perfect deference in the presence of samurai. Genuinely committed to the Celestial Order. The Perfect Land Sect considers him a significant problem because he repeatedly undoes their work. Maintains beneficial relationships with both Kazuko and Ishima despite their rivalry.

### Tamiko, Criminal Overlord
**Identity:** Unknown/Multiple Theories

Manages criminality in Hirosaka, ensuring everyone gets their cut while no one oversteps. Despite prominence in the underworld, nobody knows (or admits to knowing) who Tamiko is. Theories include: a code name for someone highly placed, Otomo Kazuko using an alias, a fiction maintained by criminal leaders, or something more-or-less-than-human. Those who defy or deceive Tamiko are invariably found dead -- or simply never found at all.

The GM can assign Tamiko's identity to an NPC or PC, leave it as a mystery, make it a shared fiction among criminals, or reveal it as another identity of Otomo Kazuko.

### Other Notable NPCs

- **Bayushi Touma:** Influential Scorpion samurai owner of the Clouds above Hirosaka sake works. Spends little time in town.
- **Itsuki:** A heimin woodcutter desperate for help because his young daughter Haya has vanished into the Haunted Grove (Another World adventure seed).
- **Keina:** A charismatic ronin treasure seeker convinced of the Lost Treasure's reality (Treasure Hunt adventure seed).
- **Mura and Yoshi:** A heimin woman and her adopted orphan son who seems to have earned the favor of the fire kami (Friend of the Kami adventure seed).
- **Watchful:** A sly ronin who owns The Grinning Mujina, actually a vampiric Shadowlands penanggalan using Hirosaka as its hunting ground (A Smell of Vinegar adventure seed).
- **Kakita Seiichi:** A Crane artisan and head of the Warai Puppet Troupe who bargained with spirits from Sakkaku for artistic success (March of the Puppets adventure seed).

---

## FIREFIGHTERS IN HIROSAKA

Groups of commoner firefighters tend to evolve into criminal gangs. Hirosaka's firefighters are brutish and disorganized street gangs, often in conflict with one another and involved in petty crimes. When their antics become excessive, Ishima has perceived ringleaders executed.

---

## LOCAL LEGENDS

### The Haunted Grove

About halfway between Hirosaka and Twin Blessings Village lies an isolated copse of trees beneath which hangs an unusual gloom. Woodcutters avoid these trees, claiming they are difficult to cut and that taking saw or ax to them provokes unsettling phenomena -- an anguished sound like a cry of pain, or blood oozing from cut wood. Those who enter may find it far larger than it appears, becoming lost for hours or days. Some have never returned.

In the heart of the copse may be an ancient overgrown shrine, a dark fathomless pool, or a deep winding cave leading to one of the Spirit Realms. Several copses could be the Haunted Grove, perhaps only at certain times.

### Lost Treasure

A popular story suggests that somewhere in town, an existing building perches atop the ruins of something far older -- perhaps predating the Empire. This ancient ruin, variously a shrine, temple, or tomb, is believed to contain fabulous treasure. Some say the ruin is protected by a malevolent curse. Interest waxes and wanes, sometimes leading townsfolk to surreptitiously poke about building foundations.

### The Great Fire

About 150 years ago, a devastating fire destroyed almost half of the town. An enduring tale describes how a lone building survived in the center of the incinerated ruins, completely untouched, and every person inside emerged unharmed. No one agrees which building it was, but if true, it potentially gained the favor of the fire kami.

---

## SUPERNATURAL ENCOUNTERS

### The Sad Little Ghost (Zashiki-Warashi)

At abandoned farms an hour southeast of Hirosaka, a zashiki-warashi -- the ghost of a young boy who fell down a disused well and died -- haunts the ruins. Not directly harmful, but desperately seeking playmates. It can charm individuals into playing with it, causing them to revert to childlike behavior. Immune to all harm. The only way to end the enchantment is to retrieve the skeletal remains from the well and give them proper funerary rites (cremation with appropriate prayers).

### A Smell of Vinegar (Penanggalan)

A series of heimin and hinin deaths by strangulation, with suspicious blood loss, leads to investigation of The Grinning Mujina sake house. The owner, a ronin named Watchful, is actually a penanggalan -- a Shadowlands creature that detaches its head and viscera to hunt, strangling victims and drinking their blood. It masks the characteristic vinegar smell through vinegar-soaked food and large barrels stored in the attic. If discovered and confronted, it fights only until it can flee.

### March of the Puppets (Sakkaku Spirits)

The Warai Puppet Troupe enjoyed a miraculous revival when patriarch Kakita Seiichi bargained with spirits from Sakkaku, the Realm of Mischief. The puppets became infused with lifelike realism, bringing fame and packed audiences. However, the puppets have begun skulking about town, committing escalating petty crimes. Seiichi seeks discreet help before the situation becomes truly egregious. The puppets are possessed by Sakkaku spirits who are mischievous by nature, not evil, but still potentially harmful and dangerous.

---

## ADVENTURE HOOKS

### Another World
A frantic heimin woodcutter named Itsuki beseeches the PCs to find his daughter Haya, whose trail leads into the Haunted Grove. Following her trail leads through a cave and into Chikushodo, the Realm of Animals. Haya is enchanted by the amazing animal spirits and does not want to leave.

### Treasure Hunt
A ronin named Keina approaches the PCs with a convincing case that the Lost Treasure tale is true, narrowed to four locations: one rice warehouse in the granaries, a courtyard behind Dwindling Fortunes, a heimin carpenter's shop, and a guesthouse behind the Temple of Shinsei. Investigation requires dealing with Seppun Ishima, Three-Scar, a heimin named Tadashi, and Hinata respectively. Keina may betray the PCs if true valuables are found.

### Friend of the Kami
A fire breaks out near the trading grounds. The PCs hear a shout for help from a burning building. Inside, they find a heimin woman Mura and her adopted son Yoshi, strangely unaffected by the fire. As they escape, the fire dies as they approach and flares behind them -- the boy Yoshi seems to be causing this. What is his true lineage, and is it connected to the building that survived the Great Fire?

### A Smell of Vinegar
Suspicious commoner deaths lead the PCs to investigate The Grinning Mujina, where the ronin owner Watchful is actually a penanggalan -- a Shadowlands monster that has been hunting in Hirosaka. The PCs must uncover and confront the creature, which will fight only to flee and can be permanently destroyed by burning its body while the head is absent.

### March of the Puppets
Kakita Seiichi, patriarch of the Warai Puppet Troupe, comes to the PCs for help. Puppets possessed by spirits from Sakkaku have been leaving the theater and committing escalating crimes around town. The PCs must deal with the spirits -- which can be fought, chased, negotiated with, or banished -- while maintaining Seiichi's secret that he bargained with unknown spirits.

---

## THE PERFECT LAND SECT IN HIROSAKA

Hinata, the abbot of the Temple of Shinsei, is the primary link to the Perfect Land Sect. He coordinates sect activities in surrounding farmlands and villages, meeting periodically with monks like Michi in Twin Blessings Village. His popular ceremonies provide cover for sect meetings. He teaches sect beliefs discreetly in the hinin encampment.

Villages are prime targets for sect recruitment. Daimyo take little interest in what villagers do as long as they pay taxes, making sect indoctrination easy to keep secret. A village taken over by the sect might display unusual crowding, prolonged chanting, meditation, martial training, and weapons storage. Such a village could serve as a base for controlling roads and rivers, pressuring local cities and castles.

Some daimyo who suspect sect control may act ruthlessly, even destroying entire villages. In Imperial territory like Hirosaka's default setting, a magistrate or investigator might be sent to look into rumors, with sect members potentially judged and executed as examples.

---

## A NOTE ABOUT HININ

In the Celestial Order, hinin are at the very bottom, considered little more than property. Their portrayal in Hirosaka reflects this cosmological hierarchy. Groups should discuss whether they wish to incorporate such elements in their play, ensuring everyone involved has a good time without being made uncomfortable.
